WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
CARTER MORGAN: We talked about
Container Builder before,

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:05.130
but we only really scratched
the surface of what it can do.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.266
In this episode of the
Cloud Rolling Update,

00:00:07.266 --> 00:00:08.640
it's time to play
with multi-step

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.340
builds in Container Builder.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.090
Special thanks, again,
to Ahmet and Kelsey

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.320
for teaching me about this.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.111
Make sure to check out
their content linked

00:00:15.111 --> 00:00:16.470
in the description below.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.070
A single Dockerfile doesn't
always get you what you need.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:21.740
And in fact, it's common
to use multiple steps

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.390
in a build pipeline to
build, test, and deploy

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.510
enterprise applications, which
means a tool that can only

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:30.960
build Docker images isn't that
useful for creating real world

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.220
applications.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.610
Luckily, Container Builder
isn't just a Docker builder.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.280
By allowing you to run multiple
build steps, each of which

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.930
can run any Docker image
as part of your build,

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:42.900
well, it provides a
simple and powerful way

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.260
to customize build steps
and build environments.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.000
You might be wondering,
why is that useful?

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.540
Well, it allows you to run
unit tests with your build

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.610
or reduce the size
of your final image

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.520
by rebaking your image
onto a leaner, smaller base

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.680
image that removes
build artifacts,

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.780
test tooling, and other crud.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.420
So to run this
feature for yourself,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.430
here's what you need to know.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:09.560
You can customize build steps
with a cloudbuild.yaml file.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.926
Each build step
runs in a container.

00:01:11.926 --> 00:01:13.550
You can bring your
own container images

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.650
or use Google-provided ones.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.440
Your repository is
mounted at /workspace.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.530
And contents of /workspace are
preserved from one build step

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:22.940
to another.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.250
And this is exactly like
Jenkins build pipelines,

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:27.770
where you have artifacts
that are stashed or unstashed

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.299
from one step of the
pipeline to the next.

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:32.090
This means that if you
distribute your apps

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.400
as containers,
well, you don't need

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.370
to host a Jenkins instance
or a third party CI service

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.650
to build and push images.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.300
So to illustrate this
better, let's use

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.860
GCB to build, test, and package
an example go application.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.440
I want to remember that
when I submit the build GCB,

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.350
my source code is pulled
into a workspace directory.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.210
From there, GCB
runs the commands

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:57.199
that I specify over the contents
of that workspace directory.

00:01:57.199 --> 00:01:59.240
It's also good to know
that the Container Builder

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:02.300
team publishes and maintains
a set of Docker containers

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.720
with common developer
tools, like get, docker,

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.830
the gcloud command
line interface.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:11.240
So I can use any of these to run
operations inside of my build.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:13.040
Now, using these
tools, I'm going

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.950
to define a
cloudbuild.yaml file.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.750
This has a pipeline
with a specific set

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.730
of steps to run, just
like in any build system.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:22.820
But instead of storing
these images locally,

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.400
we use the ones that
are stored in the cloud.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.730
First, go-install
will produce a binary

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.810
that I can use in my next step.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:32.540
Then we'll use the go
Docker container image again

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.370
to test our code.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:37.610
Finally, we'll use a Docker
container to build an image.

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:41.290
This assumes that there is a
Dockerfile in my workspace.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.990
If all of the above steps pass,
then the final result of this

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.339
will be gcb example image.

00:02:47.339 --> 00:02:49.630
When I submit the build, you
can see all of these steps

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:52.670
from the cloudbuild.yaml
file run it.

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.070
So what's the point?

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.950
Container Builder is not just
for building Docker containers.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.810
But it's a composable
ecosystem that allows

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.820
you to use any build step.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.320
For more information
about this, be

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.361
sure to check out the
links from Ahmet and Kelsey

00:03:04.361 --> 00:03:05.509
in the description below.

00:03:05.509 --> 00:03:08.050
And to learn about more advanced
features of Google Container

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.790
Builder, like using
build triggers

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:11.710
to automate creating
containers, be

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:13.780
sure to subscribe
to the channel.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:14.530
I'm Carter Morgan.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:15.880
And I'm telling you
to tune in next time

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.590
to learn what's rolling out in
the next Cloud Rolling Update.

